```python
class Label:
    def __init__(self, text):
        self.text = text

    def render(self):
        return self.text

class FramedLabel(Label):
    def render(self):
        return "[" + super().render() + "]"

plain = Label("Map")
framed = FramedLabel("Map")
print(plain.render())
print(framed.render())
```
The outputs are Map and [Map]. Label.render returns its stored string. FramedLabel.render returns a new framed string, leaving self.text unchanged.

```python
class Duration:
    def __init__(self, minutes):
        self.minutes = minutes

    @property
    def minutes(self):
        return self._minutes

    @minutes.setter
    def minutes(self, value):
        if value < 0:
            raise ValueError("minutes must be nonnegative")
        self._minutes = value

duration = Duration(10)
print(duration.minutes)
duration.minutes = 15
print(duration.minutes)
try:
    duration.minutes = -1
except ValueError:
    print(duration.minutes)
```
It prints 10, 15, 15. Construction uses self.minutes = minutes, so the same setter validates initialization too. The project supplies numeric values; deciding which other types to accept is a separate contract.

